      "updated_at": "2026-11-13T19:53:48Z",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "body": "Thanks @jQrgen, we only accept submissions that have been discussed on the mailing list or Delving Bitcoin before. I’m going to close this pull request until it has been discussed and received some interest.\r\n\r\nRegarding the document at first skim: this proposal needs more work before it should be opened here. The Motivation should explain what problem the proposal addresses and why the proposed approach improves the situation. Your list of “concerns to be addressed” includes some of the hardest parts of what a dynamic blocksize proposal would need to address to be of any interest: would adoption of the proposal guarantee CONOP to remain acceptable, would adoption be detrimental to decentralization, and is it worth to attempt a hard fork? The Rationale section should address those concerns and provide _rationale_ for why the algorithm is a good choice. “Doing the same as Nexa” is not sufficient reasoning when Nexa is not representative in any dimension for Bitcoin with fewer than four transactions per block in average.\r\n\r\nAlgorithms are a dime a dozen, the question is whether any proposed blocksize adjustment algorithm has attractive properties. E.g., it would be interesting to see some discussion how this algorithm would behave if all blocks are always full (as has been the situation for Bitcoin in 41 of the last 45 months). Further, I would suggest that any dynamic blocksize algorithm that would be considered for Bitcoin would need some mechanism that creates downward pressure on the available blockspace. I can’t imagine that any proposal which posits essentially unlimited blockspace would garner any interest.",
